From: Andrei-Alexandru Bleortu <me@andrei-z.com>
Subject: [PATCH] nss_core: validate skb reuse on 7.2

The reuse path reads only public skb state and resets a buffer the way
__alloc_skb() does (sk_buff up to tail, skb_shared_info up to dataref),
which holds on 7.2. Two checks are fixed on the way: the zerocopy test
read SKBFL_ZEROCOPY_ENABLE from tx_flags, while the SKBFL_* bits live in
shinfo->flags (skb_zcopy() reads the right field), and a page_pool skb is
no longer reused - clearing pp_recycle would keep its head from returning
to the pool.
---
--- a/nss_core.c
+++ b/nss_core.c
@@ -58,7 +58,7 @@
  * following kernel versions. Before enabling the driver in new kernels,
  * the skb recycle code must be checked against Linux skb handling.
  *
- * Tested on: 3.4, 3.10, 3.14, 3.18, 4.4, 5.4, 6.6, 6.12 and 6.18
+ * Tested on: 3.4, 3.10, 3.14, 3.18, 4.4, 5.4, 6.6, 6.12, 6.18 and 7.2
  */
 #if (!( \
 (((LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(3, 4, 0)) && (LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(3, 5, 0)))) || \
@@ -69,7 +69,8 @@
 (((LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(5, 4, 0)) && (LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(5, 5, 0)))) || \
 (((LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(6, 6, 0)) && (LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(6, 7, 0)))) || \
 (((LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(6, 12, 0)) && (LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(6, 13, 0)))) || \
-(((LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(6, 18, 0)) && (LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(6, 19, 0))))))
+(((LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(6, 18, 0)) && (LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(6, 19, 0)))) || \
+(((LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(7, 2, 0)) && (LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(7, 3, 0))))))
 #error "Check skb recycle code in this file to match Linux version"
 #endif
 
@@ -2812,7 +2813,7 @@
 	if (unlikely(skb_shinfo(nbuf)->tx_flags & SKBTX_DEV_ZEROCOPY))
 		return false;
 #else
-	if (unlikely(skb_shinfo(nbuf)->tx_flags & SKBFL_ZEROCOPY_ENABLE))
+	if (unlikely(skb_zcopy(nbuf)))
 		return false;
 #endif
 
@@ -2844,6 +2845,13 @@
 #endif
 
 	/*
+	 * A page_pool head goes back to its pool on free; reuse would clear
+	 * pp_recycle and leak it from the pool.
+	 */
+	if (unlikely(nbuf->pp_recycle))
+		return false;
+
+	/*
 	 * TODO: This check is only validated on kernel 6.6
 	 * This needs to be validated on prior linux
 	 * kernel versions.
